INV-vavup catalogued bash REDIRECTION and named the rest in its own
title -- "redirection FIRST, taint-support SECOND". This is the second
half.

Before it, the exact script the self-proof cares about emitted ONE edge,
the sink, and nothing for the read:

  secret="$API_KEY"
  echo "$secret" > /etc/cron.d/pwned
  -> bash edges: 1   calls dst=bash:redirect:0-0:>:external_symbol

So bash carried 0 sources against 3 sinks, failed INV-potuf's
both-halves predicate, and made every taint claim on ANY repository
containing a shell script `inconclusive`. Measured after: that script
goes inconclusive -> VIOLATED, and bash leaves
unsupported_taint_languages.

A CATALOGUE ALONE COULD NOT HAVE DONE IT, which is why the analyzer
comes first. Taint sources match against emitted edges; with no edge for
the read, a sources: {bash: [...]} file is a predicate no call site
passes -- inert on arrival, and measurable as a win at the catalogue
while producing zero findings. That is INV-linub's recorded lesson and
this item was filed specifically to avoid repeating it.

ONE ROW, NOT A NAME LIST, on the os.environ precedent. Python does not
enumerate environment variable names and neither can this: a curated
list is wrong the moment a repo invents a name, and wrong in the SILENT
direction. The variable actually read rides on the edge's meta for the
reader rather than being used for matching. The label is derived
(env_read -> host_secret via AUTO_SOURCE_LABEL_MAP), not hand-written,
so the two halves of the language cannot disagree about it (INV-potuf).

THE DISCRIMINATOR is a name EXPANDED but never ASSIGNED in the file,
applied WHOLE-FILE rather than per-scope: bash assignment is dynamically
scoped, so a name assigned in one function is visible in another it
calls, and a per-scope rule would call an assigned name an env read.
Erring toward FEWER sources is right for a taint source -- a missed one
under-reports, an invented one manufactures findings that do not exist.
Positional and special parameters ($1, $?) are excluded as arguments and
shell state, not environment.

Anchored to the enclosing function via the existing helper, so source
and sink share a caller (verified both ways: a function-scoped read
anchors to `dump:function`, a top-level one to the file, and in each
case the sink anchors to the same place).

One test asserted bash was unsupported and went red as intended; it now
uses markdown -- a language that genuinely has no catalogue -- so it
keeps discriminating instead of quietly asserting nothing.

